{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-54155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{2}-2,\\,y=2{t}^{4}+5$ where $t$ is a real number. Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-54155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{8{t}^{3}}{2t}=4{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(4{t}^{2})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{8t}{2t} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-54155" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8c9cde536189ca2d4109140cd6fc01bc49af801e5077510ae34c8ef04cfcfb0"} {"question" "q-54155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-54155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4"] ["math" "4t"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4t}"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{t}"] ["math" "\\frac{t}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-54155" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-78951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+3,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1.$ Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-78951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the first derivative, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\n$$\n\nTo compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2{csc}^{2}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =-2{csc}^{3}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-78951" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8c9cde536189ca2d4109140cd6fc01bc49af801e5077510ae34c8ef04cfcfb0"} {"question" "q-78951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-78951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2{csc}^{2}⁡t"] ["math" "-2{csc}^{3}⁡t"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t"] ["math" "\\operatorname{csc}⁡t"] ["math" "{sec}^{3}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-78951" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2{csc}^{3}⁡t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-78959" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve has parametric equations $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+4,\\,y=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t),$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{9}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-78959" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t),$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+4)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(4\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3t))}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{sec}^{2}⁡(3t)}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} \\\\  & =4{sec}^{3}⁡(3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{9},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}{|}_{t=\\pi /9} & =4{sec}^{3}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}} \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-78959" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8c9cde536189ca2d4109140cd6fc01bc49af801e5077510ae34c8ef04cfcfb0"} {"question" "q-78959" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-78959" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "32"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{32}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-78959" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "32"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-123003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve has parametric equations $x={t}^{2}-10,\\,y=4{t}^{6}+5.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=12{t}^{4},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-123003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=12{t}^{4},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}-10)=2t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(12{t}^{4})}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{48{t}^{3}}{2t} \\\\  & =24{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}|}_{t=1}=24\\cdot {1}^{2}=24.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-123003" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8c9cde536189ca2d4109140cd6fc01bc49af801e5077510ae34c8ef04cfcfb0"} {"question" "q-123003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-123003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-123003" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-352392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve has parametric equations $x={e}^{2t},\\,y={e}^{6t}.$ Given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{e}^{4t},$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ at the point where $t=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-352392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}},$ we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=3{e}^{4t},$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({e}^{2t})=2{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(3{e}^{4t})}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{e}^{4t}}{2{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate at $t=1,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,{|}_{t\\,=\\,1}=6{e}^{2(1)}=6{e}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/state.json" "file_sha256" "84b8648c4ce7b113de08f6750820fef74b8b686daf75eeceb0a1074ca099fdb4"} {"question" "q-352392" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978779/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8c9cde536189ca2d4109140cd6fc01bc49af801e5077510ae34c8ef04cfcfb0"} {"question" "q-352392" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "m